---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeawaterCalculator from '../../components/SeawaterCalculator.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Seawater calculator"
  description="A full-precision TEOS-10 seawater calculator: density, sound speed, enthalpy, entropy and the rest, evaluated from the Gibbs function rather than approximated."
>
  <div class="container">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/data/">Data &amp; Tools</a></p>
    <h1>Seawater calculator</h1>
    {/* Astro drops the whitespace before an inline element that begins a
        line, and keeps the whitespace after it — so a paragraph reflowed so
        that a link starts a line renders as "from theTEOS-10". Never break
        immediately before an `<a>`, `<code>` or `<em>` in this file. */}
    <p class="intro">
      Every thermodynamic property of seawater, from
      the <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/">TEOS-10</a> Gibbs function.
      Density is <code>1/g<sub>P</sub></code> here, not a fitted polynomial —
      so sound speed, heat capacity, entropy and the expansion coefficients
      are all consistent with each other and with density by construction,
      rather than to within a fit's residual.
    </p>

    <SeawaterCalculator />

    <p class="sources">
      <span>Sources</span>
      <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/">TEOS-10</a>
      <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/software.htm">GSW Oceanographic Toolbox</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/TEOS-10/GSW-C">GSW-C</a>
      <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/pubs/gsw/html/gsw_contents.html">GSW function reference</a>
    </p>

    <section class="about">
      <h2>What it does, and what that costs</h2>

      <h3>Absolute Salinity needs a position</h3>
      <p>
        The change TEOS-10 made over EOS-80 is that density depends on what the
        salt is <em>made of</em>, not only on how much conductivity it carries.
        Rivers, biology and the deep circulation leave different oceans with
        different compositions, so Absolute Salinity is Reference Salinity plus
        an anomaly that was measured rather than derived. It reaches
        0.03 g/kg in the North Pacific — about 0.024 kg/m³ of density, or
        thirty times the precision anyone quotes it to.
      </p>
      <p>
        That correction is a global lookup table, so it needs a longitude and a
        latitude. Enter them and the atlas is fetched, once, and applied.
        Leave them out and the calculator uses Reference
        Salinity <em>and says so</em> — reporting SR under SA's name without a word is
        the one mistake a page like this must not make.
      </p>

      <h3>Full precision means the Gibbs function</h3>
      <p>
        The GSW toolbox evaluates most properties from a 75-term polynomial
        fitted to specific volume, which is fast and accurate to about a part
        in 10<sup>6</sup>. This evaluates the Gibbs function itself — IAPWS-09
        for pure water, IAPWS-08 for the salt — and takes the analytic
        derivatives. The freezing point additionally needs the Gibbs function
        of ice, IAPWS-06, because it is the temperature at which the chemical
        potential of water in seawater equals that of ice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two quantities have no exact form and say so on their rows: spiciness,
        which <em>is</em> defined as a polynomial, and the reference column the
        depth conversion is built on.
      </p>

      <h3>Where it stops</h3>
      <p>
        The standard is fitted over 0 to 42 g/kg, the freezing point to 40 °C,
        and 0 to 10,000 dbar. Outside that the polynomials still return
        numbers; the calculator flags them rather than clamping, because an
        extrapolated fit is not seawater. PSS-78 itself is defined from 2 to
        42, with the Hill et al. (1986) extension below that — also flagged.
      </p>

      <h3>Checked against the reference implementation</h3>
      <p>
        The coefficients are transcribed mechanically
        from <a href="https://github.com/TEOS-10/GSW-C">GSW-C</a>, never by
        hand, and every function is checked against GSW at points spanning the
        domain. Each derivative of the Gibbs function is separately checked
        against a central difference of the one below it, over a few thousand
        states — so a mistyped digit fails a test rather than quietly shifting
        the density of the ocean. The salinity anomaly atlas reproduces GSW's
        own lookup to 2×10<sup>-19</sup>.
      </p>

      <h3>Credits</h3>
      <p>
        TEOS-10 is the work of SCOR/IAPSO Working Group 127, published by IOC,
        SCOR and IAPSO (2010) and available
        at <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/">teos-10.org</a>. The reference
        implementation is
        the <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/software.htm">Gibbs SeaWater (GSW)
        Oceanographic Toolbox</a> — this page is checked against
        its <a href="https://github.com/TEOS-10/GSW-C">C implementation</a>,
        and its <a href="https://www.teos-10.org/pubs/gsw/html/gsw_contents.html">function
        reference</a> is the place to look up anything named here.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pure-water and saline Gibbs functions are IAPWS-09 and IAPWS-08,
        and the ice one is IAPWS-06, all released by
        the <a href="https://iapws.org/">International Association for the
        Properties of Water and Steam</a>. Nothing on this page is published
        by any of them; the errors are ours.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin-block: var(--space-xl) 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-block: var(--space-xs) var(--space-md);
  }

  .intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .intro code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  /* Right under the calculator rather than only in the credits at the foot of
     a long page: "what is TEOS-10, and what is this checked against" is a
     question you have while looking at the numbers, not after reading to the
     end. */
  .sources {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-xs) var(--space-md);
    margin-block: var(--space-md) 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .sources span {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .about {
    margin-block: var(--space-2xl);
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
  }

  .about h2 {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-md);
  }

  .about h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .about p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-sm);
  }
</style>
